Materials and methods Materials and instruments Materials: THP-1 cells (Pricella, CL-0233); RPMI-1640 medium (Gibco, USA) and fetal bovine serum (Umedium, He Fei, China); Phosphate buffer solution (PBS) (Shanghai Acmec Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd, China); Cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8), 4% paraformaldehyde fix solution, primary antibody dilution buffer, secondary antibody dilution buffer, immunol staining blocking buffer, immunol staining primary antibody dilution buffer, immunol fluorescence staining secondary antibody dilution buffer, 4, 6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (DAPI) staining solution, phorbol-12-myristate-13-acetate (PMA) were purchased from Shanghai Beyotime Biotechnology Co., LTD. (China). Primary antibodies for NLRP3, cleaved caspase-1 p20, IL-1β, and β-Tubulin were purchased from Proteintech (China). Primary antibodies for ASC was purchased from Signalway Antibody LLC (USA). Primary antibodies for GSDMD-N was purchased from Cell Signaling Technology (USA). The iFluor™ 594 conjugated goat anti-mouse IgG polyclonal antibody were purchased from HuaAn Biotechnology Co., LTD. (China). CaCl 2 , NaOx and other chemical reagents were analytically pure and were purchased from Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., LTD (China). IL-1β ELISA kit and IL-18 ELISA kit were from Shanghai Dibo Biotechnology Co., LTD (China). Instruments: Zeiss LSM 800 Confocal Laser Scanning Microcopy (LSM 800, ZEISS, Germany); Inverted fluorescence microscope (Leica DMRA2, Germany); Flow cytometry (Beckman, Gallios, USA). Multifunctional microplate reader (SafireZ, Tecan, Switzerland). D/max 2400 X–ray powder diffractometer (Rigaku, Japan); Zetasizer Nano ZS laser nanoparticle size analyzer (Malvern Panalytical, UK); Microplate reader (Safire2, Tecan, Switzerland); XL-30 Environmental Scanning Electron Microscope (Phillps, Netherlands). Ultrasensitive multifunctional chemiluminescence imager (GE Amersham Imager 600, Cytiva, USA). Synthesis and characterization of COM crystals Synthesis of COM crystals According to reference [ 27 ], 100 nm COM and 10 μm COM crystals were synthesized, and 500 nm COM and 2.5 μm COM were newly synthesized on this basis. In this paper, COM crystals with different sizes were designated as COM-100 nm, COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm. The synthesis conditions are shown in Table  1 . Table 1 Synthesis conditions of COM crystals of different sizes Crystal Temperature (℃) Mixing manner Dropping time (min) Stirring time (min) c (Ca 2+ ) (mmol/L) c (Ox 2− ) (mmol/L) Stirring speed (rpm) COM-100 nm 25 Directly mixed 0 6 300 300 1250 COM-500 nm 60 Directly mixed 0 5 20 20 1200 COM-2.5 μm 60 2 drops/1 s
for 12 min
 12 2 5 5 1200 COM-10 μm 75 1 drop/2 s
for 30 min 30 2 5 5 400 COM-100 nm: At room temperature, CaCl 2  (0.6 mol/L, 50 mL) solution was quickly mixed with K 2 Ox (0.6 mol/L, 50 mL) solution, and the reaction was stirred at 1250 rpm for 6 min before centrifugation. The products were ultrasonically washed twice with absolute ethanol, filtered, and dried to obtain COM crystals with a size of about 100 nm. COM-500 nm: CaCl 2  (0.04 mol/L, 50 mL) and K 2 Ox (0.04 mol/L, 50 mL) solutions were heated to 60 ℃, mixed directly, and kept at 60 ℃ for 5 min under stirring at 1200 rpm. After standing overnight, the products were ultrasonically washed twice with absolute ethanol, filtered, and dried to obtain COM crystals with a size of about 500 nm. COM-2.5 μm: CaCl 2  (0.04 mol/L, 50 mL) solution was added to 300 mL of ultrapure water, and K 2 Ox (0.04 mol/L, 50 mL) solution was added at 60 ℃ with a dropping speed of 2 drops/1 s, and the adding time was about 12 min. The reaction was always maintained at 60 ℃ and stirred at 1200 rpm. The mixture was stirred continuously for 5 min, placed at room temperature overnight, washed twice with absolute ethanol, filtered, and dried to obtain COM crystals with a size of about 2.5 nm. COM-10 μm: Similar to synthetic COM-3 μm, but with a reduced drop rate (drop rate of 1 drop/2 s, adding time of about 30 min) and stirring speed (400 rpm). Characterization of COM crystals 
 Crystal morphology and size observed by SEM About 1 mg of COM crystals was weighed, dispersed in 10 mL of absolute ethanol, and sonicated for 3 min to form a suspension. The above suspension was dropped onto 10 mm × 10 mm slides and dried at 50 ℃. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to observe the size and morphology of COM crystals. Nano Measurer 1.2 software was used to measure the size of the photographed samples, and the statistics were normally fitted in Origin (Version 2021, OriginLab Corporation, Northampton, MA, USA) software. The size range of the sample was obtained. X-ray diffraction An appropriate amount of COM crystal samples was analyzed using an X-ray diffractometer. The test conditions were CuKᾱ rays, graphite monochromator, 30 kV, 25 mA, scanning range of 5–600, scanning speed of 80/min, and step width of 0.020/s. Cell culture THP-1 cells were cultured in RPMI-1640 medium containing 10% fetal bovine serum and 0.05 mM β-mercaptoethanol in an incubator at 37 ℃ and 5% CO 2 . The medium was changed by replenishing or partially exchanging the fluid and passaged by centrifugation. Macrophage differentiation Experimental grouping THP-1 cells were plated at a density of 10 6  cells/mL. The experiment was divided into three groups: (a) normal control group (NC): THP-1 cells were cultured in RPMI-1640 medium without serum; (b) differentiation group: THP-1 cells were induced to Mφs by incubation in complete culture medium containing 5 ng/mL PMA for 24 h, followed by resting for 24 h [ 28 ]; and (c) blank background group: serum-free RPMI-1640 medium only. Microscopic observation of cell morphology and hematoxylin–eosin staining (HE staining) The experimental groups were the same as the (a) normal control group and (b) differentiation group. The cells were seeded in 12-well plates. After differentiation, the cells were photographed under an inverted fluorescence microscope to observe the cell morphology. After cell attachment, the original culture medium was aspirated and washed once with PBS. Each well was fixed with 1.5 mL of 4% paraformaldehyde for 15 min, stained with hematoxylin staining solution for 15 min, rinsed with water to remove excess staining solution, added with eosin staining solution to stain for 90 s, rinsed with water to remove excess staining solution, observed by an inverted fluorescence microscope, and photographed. The nucleus was blue, and the cytoplasm was pink. Immunofluorescence detection of CD68 expression The experimental groups were the same as the (a) normal control group and (b) differentiation group. After cells were seeded on slides and differentiated, the cells were fixed using 4% paraformaldehyde fixative for 15 min and then washed three times with PBS. The cells were permeabilized for 10 min and washed three times with PBS. The cells were blocked with blocking solution for 1 h and washed three times with PBS. The cells were incubated at 4 ℃ overnight after dilution of the CD68 primary antibody at the prescribed ratio and washed three times with PBS. The cells were incubated for 70 min after dilution of the secondary antibody at the prescribed proportions and washed three times with PBS. The nuclei were stained with DAPI for 5 min and washed three times with PBS. Finally, a sealing operation was performed to fix the slide containing cells. The expression of CD68 in the treated cell sheets was observed by laser confocal microscopy. The images of immunofluorescence were semiquantitatively analyzed using ImageJ software. CCK-8 assay for cell viability PMA-induced differentiation of THP-1 cells into Mφs. Cells were seeded in 96-well plates. After differentiation, COM crystals were added to each well at concentrations of 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, and 2.5 mg/mL and incubated for 24 h to induce cellular damage. Subsequently, 10 μL of CCK-8 solution was added to each well of the 96-well plate, and incubation was continued for 1 h in the cell incubator. The supernatant from each well was aspirated, the absorbance was measured at 450 nm, and dual-wavelength measurements were performed using 620 nm as the reference wavelength. Each experiment was set up with five multiple wells, and the full wavelength microplate reader was used three times. Cell viability was calculated according to Eq. ( 1 ). 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
