raw:
Objective This study aimed to compare the differences in pyroptosis and polarization of macrophages induced by different sizes of calcium oxalate monohydrate (COM) crystals and discuss the mechanism of kidney stone formation.

Methods COM crystals with sizes of about 100 nm, 500 nm, 2.5 μm, and 10 μm were synthesized and characterized. THP-1 cells were induced to differentiate into macrophage-like cells. The damage of COM crystals to Mφs was evaluated by CCK-8 assay. The activation of the pyroptosis signaling pathway, cell morphology, and proportion of pyroptosis cells in Mφs induced by COM crystals were detected. Finally, the proportion of M1/M2 polarization was qualitatively and quantitatively assessed.

Results COM-100 nm, COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm all significantly induced the pyroptosis and polarization of Mφs. Among them, at the same cell viability, COM-10 μm induced the highest pyroptosis rate. The expression of NLRP3 and GSDMD-N induced by COM-10 μm increased by 81.43 ± 11.45% and 112.00 ± 9.87%, respectively, and the rate of caspase-1/PI double-positive pyroptosis cells was 22.2%. Meanwhile, COM-10 μm induced the highest proportion of M1 polarization (M1/M2 ratio was 209.90% ± 2.54%).

Conclusion COM-10 μm significantly activates the classical caspase-1-mediated pyroptosis pathway and promotes M1 polarization in Mφs. Therefore, at the same cell viability, large-sized micro-COM is more capable of inducing inflammation than nano-COM.

Graphical Abstract

Introduction The kidneys are responsible for filtering waste from the blood. Although tiny particles pass through the collecting tubules of the kidneys on a daily basis, most particles are successfully excreted through urine. When calcium oxalate monohydrate (COM) crystals grow, aggregate, and precipitate in the urinary system, and form obstruction in the collecting tubules [ 1 ], calcium oxalate stone formation may be induced. Macrophages (Mφs) are immune cells with strong phagocytic function in the human body. In the kidney, Mφs have demonstrated the ability to detect and phagocytose small particles [ 1 ]. He et al. [ 1 ] found that Mφs protrude from the inner wall of mouse renal tubules, awaiting and digesting passing particles. Okada et al. [ 2 ] demonstrated that COM crystals of 1 μm in length were degraded by lysosomes upon phagocytosis by Mφs, and this process lasted about 6 days. The key role of Mφs in COM crystallization has led to the realization that it may be possible to prevent the formation and recurrence of kidney stones in individuals by regulating the immune response [ 3 ]. Studies have shown that COM crystals activate Mφs to polarize into M1 Mφs (also known as pro-inflammatory Mφs), promote the production of inflammatory factors [ 4 ], and facilitate Mφs recruitment [ 5 ]. As a form of programmed cell death, pyroptosis has unique morphological and mechanistic characteristics and is closely related to the inflammatory response [ 6 ]. Among them, the classical pyroptosis pathway has been extensively studied. In the classical pyroptosis pathway, NOD-like receptor thermal protein domain-associated protein 3 (NLRP3), apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing CARD (ASC), and pro-caspase-1 assemble to form an inflammasome, activating pro-caspase-1, which is cleaved into p20 and p10 fragments [ 7 ]. Activated caspase-1 cleaves pro-interleukin-1 beta (pro-IL-1β) and gasdermin-D (GSDMD) into their active forms, IL-1β and the N-terminal cleavage product of gasdermin-D (GSDMD-N); GSDMD-N oligomerizes to produce membrane pores that allow the release of IL-1β from the cell [ 6 ,  7 ]. A number of studies have demonstrated the important role of pyroptosis in crystalline nephropathy. Inflammation mediated by cell–crystal interactions can lead to inflammatory damage of renal cells; promote the expression of intracellular NADPH oxidase; induce the extensive production of reactive oxygen species (ROS); activate the NLRP3 inflammasome; excrete a large number of inflammatory factors; trigger an inflammatory cascade; promote the aggregation, nucleation, and growth of COM; and eventually lead to the development of intrarenal crystals and even stones [ 8 ]. Small-sized crystals (such as nanoscale particles) are easily ingested by immune cells (such as macrophages) through endocytosis [ 9 ]; particles smaller than 1 μm are efficiently phagocytized and cleared, thereby reducing inflammatory responses [ 10 ]. Large crystals, such as micron-sized particles, are primarily internalized by immune cells via phagocytosis and frustrated phagocytosis [ 11 ], which may lead to proinflammatory responses or excessive immune activation and trigger strong proinflammatory responses by activating signaling pathways such as the NLRP3 inflammasome. In crystal-related diseases (such as gout and kidney stones), large crystal particles may be closely related to tissue damage and inflammation [ 12 ]. Chen et al. [ 13 ] showed that monosodium urate crystals are one of the pathogenic factors of gout, and long monosodium urate crystals are more toxic than short monosodium urate crystals in inducing pyroptosis in neutrophils and macrophages [ 13 ]. Small monosodium urate crystals are readily phagocytosed by macrophages but usually do not cause a strong inflammatory response. By contrast, large monosodium urate crystals stimulate macrophages to release proinflammatory cytokines such as IL-1β, which triggers a severe inflammatory response and leads to an acute gout attack. Maintaining monosodium urate crystals below 0.5 μm can prevent gout attacks [ 10 ]. Zieren et al. [ 14 ] showed that asbestos fibers need to be at least 4 μm in length to cause a significant inflammatory response [ 10 ]. In the study of fibers, Sanders et al. [ 15 ] showed that large fibers may induce a large fibrous capsule, whereas the inflammatory response of fibers below 6 μm appeared diminished. Nevertheless, the degree of inflammation varies in correlation with the dimensions of the material across different disease models, and it is influenced by the material and the cell line being studied. Breisch et al. [ 16 ] demonstrated via in vitro cell culture analysis that the size, shape, and crystal structure of granular TiO 2  particles with the same chemical properties do not influence the biological effects studied. The study by Petushkov et al. [ 17 ] indicted that the cytotoxicity of nanoscale silicalite is complex and depends on the particle size, surface functional group, and cell line used for the toxicity studies; the variations in toxicity among cell types are most likely due to the different mechanisms for nanoparticle uptake by the HEK293 and RAW264.7 cell lines. The study of Ewence et al. [ 18 ] showed that in macrophages, crystals of 1–2 μm in diameter were more potent than large crystals 15 μm) in inducing the release of proinflammatory cytokines. Sun et al. [ 19 ] showed that the toxicity of hydroxyapatite crystals to HK-2 cells increases as the crystal size decreases. The crystal size-dependent immune response may play an important role in the occurrence and development of kidney stones. However, the effect of calcium oxalate monohydrate (COM) crystal size on Mφs damage in the field of kidney stone research remains unclear, particularly regarding the phagocytic ability of Mφs on COM crystals of different sizes and the relationship between COM crystal size and Mφs pyroptosis. In this study, the effects of COM crystals with sizes of 100 nm, 500 nm, 3 μm, and 10 μm on the pyroptosis and polarization of macrophages were investigated. This work will help understand the differences in macrophage injury, inflammation, and immune response caused by different sizes of urinary microcrystals, thereby offering insights for the future application of inhibitors to regulate crystal size and design effective anti-calculus drugs. Urine contains microcrystals of different sizes and morphologies because of the difference in supersaturation of lithogenic salts, viscosity, and pH levels among individuals. Crystal size and crystal morphology can affect cytotoxicity. Our previous studies on COM crystals have shown that the difference in cytotoxicity caused by size is greater than that caused bymorphology [ 20 ,  21 ]. The formation of kidney stones includes processes such as nucleation, growth, aggregation, and adhesion of stone crystals [ 22 ]. The first step of urinary stone formation is the nucleation of urinary mineral (e.g., CaOx) from supersaturated urine. The formed nuclei (generally less than 10 nm) grow and/or aggregate to a pathological size (several tens of microns). Pathological urinary microcrystals retained in the kidney will proliferate and/or aggregate, resulting in the formation of stones (millimeter scale). Therefore, there is a close relationship between the size of urinary crystals and the formation of kidney stones. In our previous clinical investigation [ 23 ], we found that 90% of urinary crystals in healthy people were less than 20 μm, and the size was relatively uniform. However, the size of urinary crystals in patients with kidney stones varied from 1 μm to 220 μm, and the number of large urinary crystals was significantly greater than that in healthy people. Our previous analysis of clinical samples revealed the existence of calcium oxalate crystals in the kidney, ranging from the nanometer scale to the micron scale. Thus, multiple gradient sizes were selected in this paper: 100 nm, 500 nm, 2.5 μm, and 10 μm. The effect of COM crystal morphology [ 20 ] on cytotoxicity and the difference between COM crystal and calcium oxalate dihydrate (COD) crystal are also under investigation [ 24 ]. Some studies have shown that differences in material shape and morphology affect cytotoxicity, but most are due to the difference between rough and smooth surfaces of materials [ 25 ] and long needle morphology and round ball morphology [ 26 ]. Smooth titanium induces inflammatory macrophage (M1-like) activation, as indicated by increased levels of interleukins IL-1β, IL-6, and TNFα. By contrast, hydrophilic rough titanium induces macrophage activation, similar to the anti-inflammatory M2-like state, increasing interleukin IL-4 and IL-10 levels [ 25 ]. Needle-shaped carbon nanotubes and asbestos have been shown to induce IL-1β secretion from human monocyte-derived macrophages in ROS and cathepsin B/P2X in a NLRP3 activation-dependent manner. In contrast to these needle-like materials, spherical carbon black with an average size of 14 nm was ineffective for IL-1β production at the same concentration [ 25 ]. In other words, we believe that significant differences in the roughness, topography, and shape of the materials will lead to significant differences in cytotoxicity. In this study, the pyroptosis and polarization of macrophages induced by COM crystals of 100 nm, 500 nm, 3 μm, and 10 μm were investigated. Understanding the effect of urine microcrystal size on macrophage injury and inflammation is beneficial. Effective inhibitors that can regulate the size of urinary microcrystals may be developed to reduce the incidence of kidney stones.

