ated by SDS-PAGE, and the separated proteins were transferred to NC membranes and blocked with 5% skim milk for 2 h. Primary antibodies (NLRP3, ASC, GSDMD-N, pro-caspase-1, cleaved caspase-1 p20, and pro-IL-1β) were incubated overnight at 4 ℃ with slow shaking. Secondary antibodies were incubated at room temperature for 2 h. The images were developed with an ultrasensitive multifunctional chemiluminescence image. The gray values in Western blot bands were quantified using ImageJ software. Observation of the microscopic morphological characteristics of pyroptosis The experimental group was the same as the (B) same-viability group. When the incubation time was reached, the cells were washed twice with PBS, and the cell morphology was observed under a fluorescent microscope. Subsequently, cells were fixed with electron microscope fixative overnight at 4 ℃ and washed three times with PBS. The cells were dehydrated with gradient concentrations of ethanol (25%, 50%, 70%, 90%, and 100%), subjected to critical point drying with CO 2 , and sprayed with gold. Their morphology was observed by SEM. Pyroptosis observed by PI staining The experimental group was the same as the (B) same-viability group. After the incubation time, the cells were washed twice with PBS. The cells were incubated in PI working solution for 30 min at 4 ℃ and washed twice with PBS. The cells were observed under a fluorescent microscope. Casepase-1/propidium iodide (PI) double staining quantitative analysis The experimental group was the same as the (B) same-viability group. The green fluorescence probe FAM-YVAD-FMK was used to label caspase-1 in living cells [ 29 ]. After treatment, the cells were incubated at 37 ℃ in the dark for 1 h with FAM-YVAD-FMK solution, washed twice with the wash solution provided by the FLICA caspase-1 detection kit, and incubated at room temperature in the dark for 10 min with PI staining solution. Cell population was detected by flow cytometry. Characteristics of pyroptosis o