Comparison of pyroptosis induced by COM of different sizes Activation of the pyroptosis signaling pathways At the same COM crystal concentration (0.5 mg/mL), the activation of the NLRP3/ASC/caspase-1/GSDMD/IL-1β signaling pathway was observed [ 33 ]. As shown in Fig.  4 B, NLRP3, ASC, GSDMD, caspase-1, GSDMD, and IL-1β in Mφs were activated to varying degrees in the presence of 0.5 mg/mL COM crystals compared with the NC group (Fig.  4 D–I). In the COM-100 nm injury group, the contents of NLRP3, GSDMD-N, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18 were 174.0% ± 17.7%, 264.6% ± 12.0%, 347.3% ± 12.3%, and 125.4% ± 18.3% of the NC group, respectively. In the COM-10 μm injury group, the contents of NLRP3, GSDMD-N, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18 were 171.5% ± 4.6%, 276.5% ± 11.8%, 289.1% ± 2.7%, and 112.9% ± 6.1% of the NC group, respectively. At the same cell viability (70%), the expression of several pyroptosis-related proteins significantly increased in the COM-10 μm injury group. The contents of NLRP3, cleaved caspase-1 p20, GSDMD-N, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18 were 181.4% ± 11.4%, 173.5% ± 3.1%, 212.0% ± 9.9%, 482.9% ± 2.1% and 234.9% ± 5.6% of the NC group, respectively (Fig.  4 J–O). Western blot results showed that at the same cell viability (70%), the degree of activation of the classical caspase-1-dependent pathway mediated by NLRP3 was correlated with COM crystal size. The larger the COM crystal size, the greater the degree of pyroptosis. Microscopic morphology of pyroptosis Figure  5 A shows the morphological changes of Mφs during pyroptosis as observed under the light field of the microscope. In the normal control group, Mφs was round or oval with short processes, and those with active function exhibited extended elongated pseudopodia in an irregular shape. The nucleus were round, small, and dark in color. However, when the cells were damaged by COM crystal, endocytosed (red arrow) and adhered (yellow arrow) COM crystals could be observed, and the overall swelling of the cells (green arrow) was observed. Bubble-like projections (white arrows), namely, pyroptosomes, were also observed in the COM-10 μm group [ 34 ]. Fig. 5 Cell morphology of pyroptosis Mφs induced by COM crystals of different sizes.  A  Bright field observation of cell morphology. Red arrow: endocytosed COM crystal; Yellow arrow: adhered COM crystal; Green arrow: cell swelling and swelling; White arrow: Pyroptosome. Scale bar: 10 μm, n = 3.  B  Cell death and pyroptosis observed by PI staining. White arrow: pyroptosome and pyroptosis cell. Scale bar: 100 μm, n = 3.  C  Cell morphology was observed by SEM, n = 3. COM-100 nm ( b ) was amplified to observe adhesion ( f ), endocytosis ( g ) and pyroptosome ( h ); COM-500 nm ( c ) was amplified to observe adhesion ( i ), endocytosis ( j ), pyroptosome ( k ); COM-2.5 μm ( d ) was amplified to observe adhesion ( l ), endocytosis ( m ), and pyroptosome ( n ); COM-10 μm ( e ) was amplified to visualize adhesion ( o ), endocytosis ( p ), and pyroptosome ( q ). The experimental conditions were Mφs treated by different sizes of COM crystals for 24 h to 70% cell viability Cell viability was maintained at 70% for cells damaged by COM crystals in each group. Dead cells were stained with PI, and the bubbling of pyroptotic cells was observed under bright-field to identify the occurrence of pyroptosis [ 35 ]. As shown in Fig.  5 B, the cell membrane of the NC group was intact, and the number of PI-positive cells was small. After COM crystal injury, the cell membrane was seriously damaged, and the number of PI-positive cells increased. The number of PI-positive cells in the COM-100 nm group was the highest, whereas the incidence of bubbling in pyroptotic cells observed under bright field was minimal. In the COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm groups, the number of PI-positive cells increased with the increase in COM crystal size, and the incidence of bubbling of pyroptotic cells significantly increased. The number of pyroptosis cells in the COM-10 μm group was the highest than those in the other groups. Scanning electron microscopy revealed many micro-wrinkles and protrusions on the surface of normal Mφs, and the cell membrane was intact (Fig.  5 C). However, when the cells were damaged by COM crystals, endocytosed (red arrow) and adhered (yellow arrow) COM crystals could be observed, with continuous bubble production on the cell membrane surface (white arrow). Overall, the morphology of Mφs after COM crystal damage was consistent with the characteristics of pyroptosis cells [ 35 ,  36 ]. Grouping of pyroptosis cells Caspase-1 was detected by FAM-YVAD-FMK FLICA assay (green fluorescence). PI was used to stain dead cells with damaged cell membranes (red fluorescence). Hoechst 33342 was used to stain the nuclei of living cells (blue fluorescence). According to Gao et al. [ 37 ], the ratio of caspase-1 + /PI + implies caspase-1-dependent cell death in Mφs. Wree et al. [ 38 ] defined pyroptosis by the presence of active caspase-1 and PI positivity, two key features that define pyroptotic cell death, as measured by flow cytometry. As shown in Fig.  6 A, Q1 comprised caspase-1-/PI + necrotic cells, Q2 consisted of caspase-1 + /PI + late-stage pyroptotic cells [ 35 ], Q3 contained early-stage caspase-1 + /PI- pyroptosis cells [ 29 ], and Q4 included caspase-1-/PI- living cells (“ + ” denotes positive, and “-” denotes negative). Representative images of Q1, Q2, and Q3 are shown in Fig.  6 A, with red arrows indicating necrotic cells (caspase-1-/PI + /Hoechst33342 +), green arrows denoting late-stage pyroptotic cells (caspase-1 + /PI + /Hoechst33342 +), and blue arrow highlighting early-stage pyroptotic cells (caspase-1 + /PI-/Hoechst33342 +). The proportion of late-stage pyroptotic cells (Q2) [ 38 ,  39 ] in the COM crystal injury group increased with the increase in crystal size, and the order was COM-100 nm (15.7%)≈COM-500 nm (15.1%) < COM-2.5 μm (19.6%) < COM-10 μm (22.2%). The proportion (36.3%) of necrotic cells (Q1) was significantly increased in the COM-100 nm group compared with the normal control group and other COM crystal injury groups. Thus, COM-100 nm induced the highest level of cell necrosis, and COM-10 μm induced the greatest amount of pyroptosis. Fig. 6 Characteristics of Mφs pyroptosis induced by COM crystals of different sizes.  A  Dot-plots of Caspase-1 and PI by flow cytometry. Images of cells stained with Caspase-1/PI/Hoechst33342 triple staining are also provided. Green fluorescence: FAM-FLICA to detect Caspase-1; Red fluorescence: PI to detect death cells; Blue fluorescence: Hoechst33342 to locate nuclei. Red arrows point to necrotic cells (Caspase-1-/PI + /Hoechst33342 +), green arrows point to pyroptosis cells (Caspase-1 + /PI + /Hoechst33342 +), blue arrow points to pyroptosis cells at early stage (Caspase-1 + /PI-/Hoechst33342 +). Scale bar: 20 μm, n = 3.  B  IL-1β content in cell culture medium;  C  IL-18 content in cell culture medium. The experimental conditions were Mφs treated by different sizes of COM crystals for 24 h to 70% cell viability. **P < 0.01, ***P < 0.001 Release of IL-1β and IL-18 During pyroptosis, the inflammatory cytokines IL-1β and IL-18 undergo caspase-1-dependent activation and secretion. As shown in Fig.  6 B, except for the COM-100 nm group (188.1% ± 5.2% of NC group), the IL-1β release in the other COM crystal injury groups increased with increasing crystal size, in the following order: COM-500 nm (164.9% ± 7.2% of NC group) < COM-2.5 μm (166.7% ± 12.0% of NC group) < COM-10 μm (233.3% ± 13.1% of NC group). Similarly, except for the COM-100 nm group (126.1% ± 4.8% of NC group), the IL-18 release of other COM crystal groups increased with increasing crystal size (Fig.  6 C), as follows: COM-500 nm (112.5 ± 9.4% of NC group) < COM-2.5 μm (128.3 ± 5.9% of NC group) < COM-10 μm (192.3 ± 3.5% of NC group). IL-1β and IL-18 levels were elevated in the COM-100 nm group, possibly because COM-100 nm caused more necrosis than the other crystal sizes (Fig.  6 A). Necrosis is a form of death characterized by rupture of cell membranes and is closely associated with inflammation [ 39 ]. Cellular components released during necrotic death can trigger inflammation [ 39 ]. Zeta potential of COM crystals To explore the cause for the high pyroptosis rate of COM-100 nm, we measured the zeta potential of COM crystals (Fig.  7 ). Fig. 7 Zeta potential of COM crystals of different sizes.  A  Zeta potential of COM crystals dispersed in ethanol;  B  Zeta potential of COM dispersed in RPMI-1640 medium. **P < 0.01, ***P < 0.001 The absolute value of the zeta potential on the crystal surface was positively correlated with the stability of the crystal suspension. As shown in Fig.  7 A, the zeta potential values for COM-100 nm, COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm in ethanol were − 0.86 ± 0.11, − 1.73 ± 0.14, − 12.9 ± 1.5, and − 15.9 ± 1.0 mV, respectively. As the COM crystal size increased, the absolute value of its zeta potential also rose, resulting in heightened electrostatic repulsion between crystals, which diminished their tendency to aggregate and enhanced the stability of the crystal suspension. As shown in Fig.  7 B, the zeta potential values of the four COM crystals were − 11.00 ± 0.35, − 8.75 ± 0.54, − 13.30 ± 0.45, and − 14.267 ± 0.92 mV, respectively. In general, the absolute value of its zeta potential increases with the increase in crystal size. However, the COM-100 nm crystals demonstrated anomalies, possibly due to the enhanced aggregation in RPMI-1640 medium. Polarization ratio of Mφs during pyroptosis To link the pyroptosis of Mφs with the inflammatory process, we investigated the polarization of Mφs damaged by COM crystals. Typical Mφs are characterized by a wrinkled, round, or oval morphology, and some cells have protruding pseudopods [ 40 ]. M1-type Mφs are diverse, characterized by abundant pseudopods, obvious forking, or morphology resembling a fried egg [ 40 ,  41 ]. M2-type Mφs are uniform in shape, predominantly resembling elongated strip-like fusiform structures, with no obvious bifurcation [ 41 ]. Under SEM (Fig.  8 A), the cells in the normal control group showed typical characteristics of Mφs, whereas the cells in the COM crystal group showed a gradual decrease in the number of M2 type and a gradual increase in the number of M1 type as the COM crystal size increased. Fig. 8 Qualitative Analysis of Mφs polarization induced by COM crystals of different sizes.  A  Cell morphology observed by SEM. Red arrow: endocytic COM crystal; Yellow arrows: adhered COM crystals. The expression of ( B ) CD86, ( C ) iNOS, ( D ) CD163, ( E ) CD206 detected by immunofluorescence. The experimental conditions were Mφs treated by different sizes of COM crystals for 24 h to 70% cell viability. Scale bar: 20 μm, n = 3 By detecting markers of M1 Mφs (CD86 and iNOS) and M2 Mφs (CD163 and CD206) [ 42 ,  43 ], the ratio of M1/M2 Mφs was analyzed. As shown in Fig.  8 B, C, we found that COM crystals of all sizes could significantly increase the contents of CD86 and iNOS, and the contents of CD86 and iNOS gradually increased with the increase in COM crystal size. As shown in Fig.  8 D, E, COM crystals of all sizes could significantly induce the increase in the CD163 and CD206 contents, and the contents of CD163 and CD206 gradually decreased with the increase in COM crystal size. Thus, with the increase in COM crystal size, M1 Mφs gradually increased and M2 Mφs gradually decreased. Flow cytometry was further used to quantitatively analyze the polarization ratio of Mφs, as shown in Fig.  9 A, C. COM crystals of all sizes could significantly enhance the CD86 content, which increased with the increase in COM crystal size. The order was COM-100 nm (42.2%) < COM-500 nm (47.2%) < COM-2.5 μm (57.5%) < COM-10 μm (62.8%). Fig. 9 Quantitative Analysis of the proportion of Mφs polarizations induced by COM crystals of different sizes.  A  CD86 expression analyzed by flow cytometry, n = 3;  B  Flow cytometry analysis of CD206 expression, n = 3;  C  Statistical analysis of ( A );  D  Statistical analysis of ( B );  E  The ratio of M1/M2 cells. The experimental conditions were Mφs treated by different sizes of COM crystals for 24 h to 70% cell viability. *P < 0.05, **P < 0.01, ***P < 0.001 COM crystals of varying sizes can significantly enhance the CD206 content (Fig.  9 B, D), and the CD206 content gradually decreased with the increase COM crystal size. The order was COM-100 nm (43.3%) > COM-500 nm (39.2%) > COM-2.5 μm (36.1%) > COM-10 μm (28.5%). Figure  9 E quantitatively assesses the M1/M2 polarization ratio induced by COM crystals. The results showed that as the size of COM crystals increased, the proportion of M1/M2 cells induced by COM crystals gradually increased, as follows: COM-100 nm (94.0% ± 2.9% of NC) > COM-500 nm (118.7% ± 5.5% of NC) > COM-2.5 μm (153.4% ± 6.0% of NC) > COM-10 μm (209.9% ± 2.5% of NC). The results were consistent with Fig.  8 , that is, with the increase in COM crystal size, the M1 type gradually increased and the M2 type gradually decreased. Among them, the proportion of M1/M2 cells (94.0% ± 2.9% of NC) was slightly lower or closer than that of the NC group because of the slightly more or closer M2-type polarization induced by COM-100 nm.

Activation of the pyroptosis signaling pathways At the same COM crystal concentration (0.5 mg/mL), the activation of the NLRP3/ASC/caspase-1/GSDMD/IL-1β signaling pathway was observed [ 33 ]. As shown in Fig.  4 B, NLRP3, ASC, GSDMD, caspase-1, GSDMD, and IL-1β in Mφs were activated to varying degrees in the presence of 0.5 mg/mL COM crystals compared with the NC group (Fig.  4 D–I). In the COM-100 nm injury group, the contents of NLRP3, GSDMD-N, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18 were 174.0% ± 17.7%, 264.6% ± 12.0%, 347.3% ± 12.3%, and 125.4% ± 18.3% of the NC group, respectively. In the COM-10 μm injury group, the contents of NLRP3, GSDMD-N, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18 were 171.5% ± 4.6%, 276.5% ± 11.8%, 289.1% ± 2.7%, and 112.9% ± 6.1% of the NC group, respectively. At the same cell viability (70%), the expression of several pyroptosis-related proteins significantly increased in the COM-10 μm injury group. The contents of NLRP3, cleaved caspase-1 p20, GSDMD-N, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18 were 181.4% ± 11.4%, 173.5% ± 3.1%, 212.0% ± 9.9%, 482.9% ± 2.1% and 234.9% ± 5.6% of the NC group, respectively (Fig.  4 J–O). Western blot results showed that at the same cell viability (70%), the degree of activation of the classical caspase-1-dependent pathway mediated by NLRP3 was correlated with COM crystal size. The larger the COM crystal size, the greater the degree of pyroptosis.

