itive cells was small. After COM crystal injury, the cell membrane was seriously damaged, and the number of PI-positive cells increased. The number of PI-positive cells in the COM-100 nm group was the highest, whereas the incidence of bubbling in pyroptotic cells observed under bright field was minimal. In the COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm groups, the number of PI-positive cells increased with the increase in COM crystal size, and the incidence of bubbling of pyroptotic cells significantly increased. The number of pyroptosis cells in the COM-10 μm group was the highest than those in the other groups. Scanning electron microscopy revealed many micro-wrinkles and protrusions on the surface of normal Mφs, and the cell membrane was intact (Fig.  5 C). However, when the cells were damaged by COM crystals, endocytosed (red arrow) and adhered (yellow arrow) COM crystals could be observed, with continuous bubble production on the cell membrane surface (white arrow). Overall, the morphology of Mφs after COM crystal damage was consistent with the characteristics of pyroptosis cells [ 35 ,  36 ].

