Release of IL-1β and IL-18 During pyroptosis, the inflammatory cytokines IL-1β and IL-18 undergo caspase-1-dependent activation and secretion. As shown in Fig.  6 B, except for the COM-100 nm group (188.1% ± 5.2% of NC group), the IL-1β release in the other COM crystal injury groups increased with increasing crystal size, in the following order: COM-500 nm (164.9% ± 7.2% of NC group) < COM-2.5 μm (166.7% ± 12.0% of NC group) < COM-10 μm (233.3% ± 13.1% of NC group). Similarly, except for the COM-100 nm group (126.1% ± 4.8% of NC group), the IL-18 release of other COM crystal groups increased with increasing crystal size (Fig.  6 C), as follows: COM-500 nm (112.5 ± 9.4% of NC group) < COM-2.5 μm (128.3 ± 5.9% of NC group) < COM-10 μm (192.3 ± 3.5% of NC group). IL-1β and IL-18 levels were elevated in the COM-100 nm group, possibly because COM-100 nm caused more necrosis than the other crystal sizes (Fig.  6 A). Necrosis is a form of death characterized by rupture of cell membranes and is closely associated with inflammation [ 39 ]. Cellular components released during necrotic death can trigger inflammation [ 39 ].

Zeta potential of COM crystals To explore the cause for the high pyroptosis rate of COM-100 nm, we measured the zeta potential of COM crystals (Fig.  7 ). Fig. 7 Zeta potential of COM crystals of different sizes.  A  Zeta potential of COM crystals dispersed in ethanol;  B  Zeta potential of COM dispersed in RPMI-1640 medium. **P < 0.01, ***P < 0.001 The absolute value of the zeta potential on the crystal surface was positively correlated with the stability of the crystal suspension. As shown in Fig.  7 A, the zeta potential values for COM-100 nm, COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm in ethanol were − 0.86 ± 0.11, − 1.73 ± 0.14, − 12.9 ± 1.5, and − 15.9 ± 1.0 mV, respectively. As the COM crystal size increased, the absolute value of its zeta potential also rose, resulting in heightened electrostatic repulsion between crystals, which diminished their tendency to aggregate and enhanced the stability of the crystal suspension. As shown in Fig.  7 B, the zeta potential values of the four COM crystals were − 11.00 ± 0.35, − 8.75 ± 0.54, − 13.30 ± 0.45, and − 14.267 ± 0.92 mV, respectively. In general, the absolute value of its zeta potential increases with the increase in crystal size. However, the COM-100 nm crystals demonstrated anomalies, possibly due to the enhanced aggregation in RPMI-1640 medium.

