the polarization ratio of Mφs, as shown in Fig.  9 A, C. COM crystals of all sizes could significantly enhance the CD86 content, which increased with the increase in COM crystal size. The order was COM-100 nm (42.2%) < COM-500 nm (47.2%) < COM-2.5 μm (57.5%) < COM-10 μm (62.8%). Fig. 9 Quantitative Analysis of the proportion of Mφs polarizations induced by COM crystals of different sizes.  A  CD86 expression analyzed by flow cytometry, n = 3;  B  Flow cytometry analysis of CD206 expression, n = 3;  C  Statistical analysis of ( A );  D  Statistical analysis of ( B );  E  The ratio of M1/M2 cells. The experimental conditions were Mφs treated by different sizes of COM crystals for 24 h to 70% cell viability. *P < 0.05, **P < 0.01, ***P < 0.001 COM crystals of varying sizes can significantly enhance the CD206 content (Fig.  9 B, D), and the CD206 content gradually decreased with the increase COM crystal size. The order was COM-100 nm (43.3%) > COM-500 nm (39.2%) > COM-2.5 μm (36.1%) > COM-10 μm (28.5%). Figure  9 E quantitatively assesses the M1/M2 polarization ratio induced by COM crystals. The results showed that as the size of COM crystals increased, the proportion of M1/M2 cells induced by COM crystals gradually increased, as follows: COM-100 nm (94.0% ± 2.9% of NC) > COM-500 nm (118.7% ± 5.5% of NC) > COM-2.5 μm (153.4% ± 6.0% of NC) > COM-10 μm (209.9% ± 2.5% of NC). The results were consistent with Fig.  8 , that is, with the increase in COM crystal size, the M1 type gradually increased and the M2 type gradually decreased. Among them, the proportion of M1/M2 cells (94.0% ± 2.9% of NC) was slightly lower or closer than that of the NC group because of the slightly more or closer M2-type polarization induced by COM-100 nm.

Discussion Crystal–cell interactions: processes and molecular mechanisms The interaction between COM crystals and cells is a dynamic process that includes initial adhesion, endocytosis or shedding of the crystals, and downstream response. COM crystal–cell adhesion: molecular mechanisms. The adherence of COM crystals to cells involves a variety of biophysical mechanisms and molecular components. The charge interaction mediates the binding of COM crystal to the cell membrane. This electrostatic attraction is the main driving force for adhesion [ 44 ]. COM crystals with a large Ca 2+  ion-rich ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
