orted that 50 mM calcium markedly promotes crystal adhesion to cellular proteins [ 56 ]. Xiang et al. demonstrated that Ca 2+  treatment increases the expression levels of pyroptosis and adhesion-related proteins [ 57 ]. A pH range of 4 or 5 is optimal for the binding of COM crystals to cellular proteins [ 56 ]. A urine pH at the low limit observed in humans, i.e., pH 5 (or even slightly less), may predispose individuals to kidney stone formation by increasing the likelihood of calcium oxalate crystal adhesion to cells and subsequent retention in the kidneys. Therefore, hypercalciuria and/or an acid urine may promote renal stone formation via unexpected pathways (i.e., crystal binding to cells rather than crystal growth) [ 56 ]. The size of COM crystals can also affect the strength of their interaction with cell membranes. Peerapen et al. [ 47 ] demonstrated that COM crystals of varying sizes exhibit differential adhesive capabilities with renal tubular epithelial cells. Large crystals showed enhanced capability to bind onto the epithelial cell surface. Their findings were consistent with the clinical findings showing that the large COM crystals were most pathogenic and were found only in the urine of stone formers [ 47 ]. Cellular injury may promote the interaction of COM crystals with cells. Individual cell injury (loss of lipid asymmetry) and generalized cell monolayer injury (loss of cell polarity) result in altered cell surface presentations, and both forms of injury result in an increased affinity for crystal attachment [ 58 ]. The crystal-binding capability of injured Vero cells was obviously stronger than that of the control group because of the increased expression of negatively charged adhesion molecules, such as OPN [ 59 ], in the injured Vero cells. Endocytosis of COM crystals by cells: molecular mechanisms. There are significant differences in the endocytic mechanisms of COM crystals with different sizes. Nanoscale COM crystals such as 50 and 100 nm may rely on clathrin-mediated endocytosis and caveolin-dependent endocytosis [ 9 ]. Large micron-sized crystals may be ingested via macropinocytosis or phagocytosis [ 9 ,  11 ]. The “frustrated phagocytosis” of large particles (the inability of phagocytes to engulf these particles) fosters the formation of giant cells, a polyploid version of proinflammatory macrophages [ 11 ]. The endocytosed COM crystals are encapsulated within early endosomes and then fused with lysosomes. The acidic environment of the lysosome may promote crystal dissolution, but excessive crystal accumulation cannot be efficiently digested by the lysosome, thereby destabilizing the lysosome and inducing cellular stress and protease leakage into the cytoplasm [ 11 ,  60 ]. COM crystals interact differently with various cell types. Different cell types also vary in their ability to interact with COM crystals. Renal proximal tubular cells are highly sensitive to COM crystals [ 61 ]. Collecting duct cells exhibit weak binding affinity for crystals [ 61 ]. Macrophages remove crystals mainly through phagocytosis [ 5 ]. COM crystal-induced pyroptosis and polarization: size effect 
 COM crystal-induced pyroptosis: the process. COM crystals rapidly bind to the cell membrane, after which cells internalize the crystals through endocytosis or phagocytosis [ 11 ]. The cell membrane integrity of cells adhered by crystals is destroyed, resulting in the eversion of phosphatidylserine and the exposure of adhesion molecules (such as osteopontin OPN and CD44), forming new crystal binding sites and forming a vicious cycle of adhesion–injury–adhesion [ 59 ]. Crystals that are endocytosed by cells cause organelle damage, such as lysosomal damage, mitochondrial damage, and endoplasmic reticulum damage; induce ROS overproduction; activate NLRP3 inflammasome; and promote the release of inflammatory factors [ 62 ]; all of these changes lead to pyroptosis. Adhesion and endocytosis can lead to cell damage. The damage intensity of endocytic crystals is generally higher than that of crystal adhesion due to the direct destruction of key organelles and amplification of oxidative stress and apoptosis signals. In our previous study [ 63 ], we observed that renal epithelial cells are capable of endocytosing COM crystals with a particle size ≤ 3 μm. By contrast, this study showed that macrophages could endocytose intact COM-10 μm, and the large size of COM crystals rendered them difficult to degrade (Fig.  5 C). Therefore, the inflammatory response of renal tubular epithelial cells (HK-2) and macrophages (derived from THP-1) to COM crystals differed. COM crystal-induced pyroptosis: size effect. At the same concentration (0.5 mg/mL COM crystals), COM-100 nm induced pyroptosis more significantly than the other sizes (Fig.  4 B). The reasons may be as follows. (1) The dispersed nanocrystals undergo extensive endocytosis, facilitating their transport to the lysosome, where they are degraded into oxalate ions and calcium ions. This process alters the ionic strength of the lysosome, disrupts osmotic pressure equilibrium, and destroys lysosomal integrity [ 64 ]. (2) The more negative zeta potential indicates (Fig.  7 B, compared with COM-500 nm) that COM-100 nm is predisposed to aggregation, so the actual particle size in contact with the cell is not uniform. (3) In the nanometer range, particles behave similar to chemicals, making surface chemistry very important [ 10 ]. (4) In addition, we found that the proportion of necrotic cells was high in the COM-100 nm group, and whether necrosis will interfere with the expression of pyroptosis-related indicators remains unclear. In general, there is some signal crosstalk between pyroptosis and necrosis [ 65 ], as well as some degree of crosstalk between multiple modes of cell death [ 66 ]. In subsequent studies, the cause of pyroptosis can be investigated by using necrosis inhibitors to inhibit cell necrosis [ 67 ]. However, at the same concentration (0.5 mg/mL COM crystals), large COM crystals induced a high cell survival rate (Fig.  4 A), so we believe that comparing the pyroptosis rate of COM crystals of different sizes at this time is inappropriate. Therefore, pyroptosis was detected at 70% cell viability, and the results showed that COM-10 μm significantly induced pyroptosis (Fig.  4 C). At the same cell viability (70% cell viability), the degree of pyroptosis induced by COM-500 nm, COM-2.5 μm, and COM-10 μm showed a significant size effect, that is, except for COM-100 nm, the larger the size, the more severe the pyroptosis and the higher the proportion of pyroptotic cells (Figs.  4 ,  5 ,  6 ). Figure  10  summarizes the damage and induced polarization of Mφs by small (< 1 μm) and large (> 1 μm) COM crystals. Moreover, for nanocrystals, a single cell is sufficient for phagocytosis; however, when the size of crystals is too large for a single cell to phagocytose them, multiple cells can act together [ 11 ,  68 ]. Thus, in Fig.  10 , multiple nanocrystals interact with one cell, and one or more microcrystals interact with one or more cells. A time-dependent reduction in crystal size was revealed in cells [ 69 ]. Therefore, we hypothesized that nano-scale crystals may be more susceptible to lysosomal degradation, whereas micron-scale crystals may remain in the cell for a long time, triggering a persistent inflammatory response. In addition, endocytosis of micron-scale crystals may be accompanied with the activation of inflammatory signaling pathways, promoting cell damage and crystal retention. The inability to digest microparticles inside lysosomes promotes lysosomal instability and leakage, which serves as an intracellular danger signal that triggers inflammation and cell death [ 11 ]. Many studies have focused on reducing the size of kidney stones to reduce the degree of kidney damage and prevent the deposition of calcium oxalate crystals in the kidneys [ 69 – 72 ]. Hydroxymalonic acid, a small-molecule compound, can bind to the surface of COM crystals through specific intermolecular interactions, inhibit the growth of crystals, impede renal calcium oxalate crystal deposition, and reduce kidney injury, demonstrating the feasibility of crystal growth inhibitors for the prevention and treatment of kidney stones [ 70 ]. Tamm–Horsfall protein (at 0.4–40 μg/mL) reduced the COM crystal size in a concentration-dependent manner [ 71 ]. Diosmin significantly increased the mount and mass of COM crystals during crystallization but reduced the crystal size and growth [ 72 ]. Cell or tissue damage is a prerequisite for stone formation [ 73 ]. Therefore, studying the damage of Mφs caused by COM crystals of different sizes helps to understand the formation process of kidney stones and reasonably explains the feasibility of reducing the size of COM crystals to prevent the development of kidney stones. COM crystal-induced polarization: size effect. The proportion of Mφs polarized into M1/M2 cells induced by COM crystals of different sizes was compared by detecting cell morphology and the expression levels of CD86, iNOS, CD163, and CD206. At 70% cell viability, COM crystal-induced polarization showed a significant size effect, that is, a larger size resulted in a higher proportion of M1-type cells and a lower proportion of M2-type cells (Figs.  8 ,  9 ). Inflammation and Mφs infiltration are typical processes associated with kidney stone formation [ 74 ], and renal or peripheral Mφs can migrate to sites of crystal deposition and phagocytose the crystals [ 75 ]. Crystals interact with Mφs, inducing their polarization and contributing to inflammatory responses. M1 and M2 Mφs have different regulatory roles in the formation of kidney stones [ 76 ]. M1 Mφs can promote the development of inflammation, and long-term local inflammation can promote the formation of kidney stones [ 75 ]. By contrast, M2 Mφs has anti-inflammatory and crystal phagocytic abilities, which can reduce renal CaOx crystal sharpening [ 77 ,  78 ], thereby lowering the risk of kidney stone formation. Fig. 10 Mechanism of nano/micron COM crystals damage Mφs and induce pyroptosis and polarization Association of pyroptosis and polarization At the same cell viability (70%), COM-10 μm induced the most severe pyroptosis, the highest proportion of pyroptosis cells, and the highest proportion of polarization to M1 Mφs compared with the other sizes. There may be crosstalk or cross-regulation between pyroptosis and polarization. Macrophage pyroptosis and polarization often occur simultaneously. Macrophage polarization and pyroptosis share common binding sites, signaling pathways, and outcomes [ 79 ]. The accumulation of ROS is an important co-trigger of pyroptosis and polarization [ 79 – 82 ]. NLRP3 inflammasome activation can be induced by mitochondrial ROS generation, mitochondrial dysfunction, and oxidized mitochondrial DNA [ 80 ], followed by pyroptosis. Elevated ROS levels mainly facilitate the phagocytic activity of M1 macrophages [ 81 ]. As a second messenger, ROS mediates the inflammatory response of M1 macrophages, which is primarily activated by MAPK, NF-κB, and inflammasomes [ 82 ]. The NLRP3 inflammasome is the core molecule linking pyroptosis and polarization [ 27 ,  83 ]. NLRP3 can directly activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, activate GSDMD, cleave caspase-1, and activate and release IL-1β and IL-18, leading to pyroptosis [ 33 ]. NLRP3 can also promote the polarization of macrophages to M1 type by converting danger recognition into danger response by activating caspase 1, which cleaves pro-IL-1β into its bioactive and secreted form and promotes macrophage polarization into the proinflammatory M1 phenotype [ 11 ]. Although the mechanism of the above process has not been fully explored, many studies corroborate it. Inhibition of NLRP3 and ASC in mice fed a high oxalate diet was found to reduce CaOx crystal deposition in the kidneys and alter the phenotype of renal infiltrating Mφs from an M1 pro-inflammatory phenotype to an M2 anti-inflammatory phenotype compared with untreated controls [ 84 ]. The TLR/NF-κB pathway not only plays an important role in M1 polarization but also induces pyroptosis [ 85 ,  86 ]. Knockdown of CD38 promotes the expression of molecules related to M1 polarization and triggers severe pyroptosis [ 87 ]. In mice with sepsis-induced acute lung injury, alveolar macrophages are polarized along with pyroptosis. Alveolar macrophage pyroptosis is related to the polarization of alveolar Mφ to M1 type, and inhibition of pyroptosis can inhibit the polarization of alveolar Mφs to M1 type [ 88 ]. Therapeutic strategies targeting pyroptosis or polarization Despite the successful advancement of shock wave lithotripsy and endoscopic technology in the treatment of kidney stones, the incidence and recurrence rate of kidney stones remain high. The etiology and formation mechanisms of kidney stones have not been fully elucidated, and effective prevention methods are lacking in clinical practice. At present, citrate, allopurinol, magnesium, and Chinese herbal medicine are often used as adjuvant treatments for kidney stones, but their therapeutic effect is limited. In recent years, researchers have suppressed the persistence of inflammation in various disease models by decreasing pyroptosis and M1 polarization to inhibit diseases [ 88 – 91 ]. However, this treatment strategy has rarely been mentioned in the prevention and treatment of kidney stones and may guide our future research endeavors. The experimental results of this study showed that inhibiting pyroptosis and regulating the polarization direction are viable techniques for the prevention and treatment of kidney stones. Ba et al. [ 92 ] presented a novel approach using engineered macrophage cell membrane-coated hollow mesoporous manganese dioxide nanoparticles (KM@M@M) to target and treat COM-induced kidney injury, effectively scavenge ROS, and inhibit NLRP3 inflammasome activation, thereby preventing pyroptosis and kidney damage. Ding et al. [ 93 ] showed that vitexin exerts protective effects against nephrolithiasis by inhibiting pyroptosis activation, apoptosis, EMT, and macrophage infiltration. This work studied the mechanism of kidney stone formation from the perspective of calcium oxalate crystal-induced macrophage pyroptosis and polarization, offering insights for the prevention of calcium oxalate formation and the design of targeted anti-stone drugs.

