Association of pyroptosis and polarization At the same cell viability (70%), COM-10 μm induced the most severe pyroptosis, the highest proportion of pyroptosis cells, and the highest proportion of polarization to M1 Mφs compared with the other sizes. There may be crosstalk or cross-regulation between pyroptosis and polarization. Macrophage pyroptosis and polarization often occur simultaneously. Macrophage polarization and pyroptosis share common binding sites, signaling pathways, and outcomes [ 79 ]. The accumulation of ROS is an important co-trigger of pyroptosis and polarization [ 79 – 82 ]. NLRP3 inflammasome activation can be induced by mitochondrial ROS generation, mitochondrial dysfunction, and oxidized mitochondrial DNA [ 80 ], followed by pyroptosis. Elevated ROS levels mainly facilitate the phagocytic activity of M1 macrophages [ 81 ]. As a second messenger, ROS mediates the inflammatory response of M1 macrophages, which is primarily activated by MAPK, NF-κB, and inflammasomes [ 82 ]. The NLRP3 inflammasome is the core molecule linking pyroptosis and polarization [ 27 ,  83 ]. NLRP3 can directly activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, activate GSDMD, cleave caspase-1, and activate and release IL-1β and IL-18, leading to pyroptosis [ 33 ]. NLRP3 can also promote the polarization of macrophages to M1 type by converting danger recognition into danger response by activating caspase 1, which cleaves pro-IL-1β into its bioactive and secreted form and promotes macrophage polarization into the proinflammatory M1 phenotype [ 11 ]. Although the mechanism of the above process has not been fully explored, many studies corroborate it. Inhibition of NLRP3 and ASC in mice fed a high oxalate diet was found to reduce CaOx crystal deposition in the kidneys and alter the phenotype of renal infiltrating Mφs from an M1 pro-inflammatory phenotype to an M2 anti-inflammatory phenotype compared with untreated controls [ 84 ]. The TLR/NF-κB pathway not only plays an important role in M1 polarization but also induces pyroptosis [ 85 ,  86 ]. Knockdown of CD38 promotes the expression of molecules related to M1 polarization and triggers severe pyroptosis [ 87 ]. In mice with sepsis-induced acute lung injury, alveolar macrophages are polarized along with pyroptosis. Alveolar macrophage pyroptosis is related to the polarization of alveolar Mφ to M1 type, and inhibition of pyroptosis can inhibit the polarization of alveolar Mφs to M1 type [ 88 ].

Therapeutic strategies targeting pyroptosis or polarization Despite the successful advancement of shock wave lithotripsy and endoscopic technology in the treatment of kidney stones, the incidence and recurrence rate of kidney stones remain high. The etiology and formation mechanisms of kidney stones have not been fully elucidated, and effective prevention methods are lacking in clinical practice. At present, citrate, allopurinol, magnesium, and Chinese herbal medicine are often used as adjuvant treatments for kidney stones, but their therapeutic effect is limited. In recent years, researchers have suppressed the persistence of inflammation in various disease models by decreasing pyroptosis and M1 polarization to inhibit diseases [ 88 – 91 ]. However, this treatment strategy has rarely been mentioned in the prevention and treatment of kidney stones and may guide our future research endeavors. The experimental results of this study showed that inhibiting pyroptosis and regulating the polarization direction are viable techniques for the prevention and treatment of kidney stones. Ba et al. [ 92 ] presented a novel approach using engineered macrophage cell membrane-coated hollow mesoporous manganese dioxide nanoparticles (KM@M@M) to target and treat COM-induced kidney injury, effectively scavenge ROS, and inhibit NLRP3 inflammasome activation, thereby preventing pyroptosis and kidney damage. Ding et al. [ 93 ] showed that vitexin exerts protective effects against nephrolithiasis by inhibiting pyroptosis activation, apoptosis, EMT, and macrophage infiltration. This work studied the mechanism of kidney stone formation from the perspective of calcium oxalate crystal-induced macrophage pyroptosis and polarization, offering insights for the prevention of calcium oxalate formation and the design of targeted anti-stone drugs.

Conclusions This study demonstrated that COM crystals induce pyroptosis in Mφs by activating the NLRP3/ASC/caspase-1/GSDMD/IL-1β signaling pathway. The large size of COM crystals is correlated with an elevated rate of pyroptosis and enhanced M1 polarization, indicating that large COM crystals are effective in inducing pyroptosis and M1 polarization and promoting inflammation. This study elucidated the mechanism by which large-size COM crystals induce inflammation, thereby enhancing the understanding of the formation of kidney stones. The feasibility of reducing the dimensions of COM crystals and suppressing M1 Mφs to prevent the development of kidney stones was also explained. This work provides theoretical support for the rational design of Mφ-targeted drugs for the prevention and treatment of stones. However, this study had some limitations. Although we examined the effect of crystal size on cytotoxicity, we must acknowledge that the potential influence of crystal morphology on Mφs response is unavoidable.
